WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
MONEY LOSING GIANTS:
Being profitable is virtually useless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:09.600
for modern companies. Founder’s don’t care about&nbsp;
profit, shareholders don’t care about profit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:17.400
and even employees don’t care about profit. This&nbsp;
has resulted in an era of money losing giants. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:25.980
the last 12 months, Zillow lost $101 million.&nbsp;
WeWork lost $2.7 billion. Snapchat lost $1.43&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:33.720
billion. And Uber lost, wait for it, $9.1 billion.&nbsp;
Traditionally, such losses would be a nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:40.680
for owners. If they don’t turn around the company&nbsp;
soon, bankruptcy would be a high probability. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:48.720
modern companies don’t really care. What they all&nbsp;
care about instead is growth and lots of it. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:54.960
long as the company is growing, any loss is deemed&nbsp;
acceptable. It’s not just these new startups that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.480
have been going down this path either. Tech giants&nbsp;
have been using this strategy for decades. Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.720
for example, has always teetered on the edge&nbsp;
of profitability, and their retail business is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:13.380
still unprofitable to this day. A similar story&nbsp;
can be said about Google. Google is a cash cow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:19.620
but that’s only due to one business, their&nbsp;
ad business. Virtually everything else is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:25.740
a loss. Their cloud business, for example, lost&nbsp;
$480 million last quarter. And as for Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.940
I mean, do I even have to say&nbsp;
anything about the Metaverse?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.940
Some more legacy company have also turned to this&nbsp;
strategy in recent times like Disney. While Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.560
is still profitable as a company, they’re&nbsp;
definitely using the modern approach when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.900
comes to new services like Disney+. Despite&nbsp;
having hundreds of millions of subscribers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:52.980
Disney streaming lost $1.5 billion in the last&nbsp;
quarter alone. And it’s not like these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:59.160
are forced into losses. Most of them could&nbsp;
raise prices, cut marketing, lower reinvestments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:05.940
and become profitable basically overnight.&nbsp;
But, they actively choose not to because 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:12.060
they can afford it, and 2, they actually think&nbsp;
that’s it’s the better play long term. It wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:18.060
always like this though. Back in the day, profit&nbsp;
was king. Owners needed profits to pay themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.160
They needed profits to pay shareholders and&nbsp;
they needed profits to give employees bonuses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.840
and additional perks. So, whether you were&nbsp;
running an oil business or a steel business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:34.320
the goal was to achieve profitability as&nbsp;
quickly as possible. But, over time, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:40.140
urgency to reach profitability slowly withered&nbsp;
away. Instead of having 3, 6, or 12 month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:47.160
timelines to reach profitability, owners started&nbsp;
having 2, 5, 10 year long timeframes. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:53.520
it seems like this timeframe has expanded to&nbsp;
encompass infinity. So, here’s the story of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.280
everyone involved in a company from shareholders&nbsp;
to employees slowly accepted indefinite losses.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.900
FOUNDERS SUCCUMB:&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:10.140
While this acceptance of losses may seem like&nbsp;
a new trend, it’s actually been developing for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:16.200
a 100 years and dates back to WW1. Such large&nbsp;
scale wars are naturally full nation efforts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.420
so it’s not surprising that the government&nbsp;
expected everyone to step up and offer their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.760
knowledge and expertise to the government. This of&nbsp;
course included companies and their executives who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.380
were especially important in scaling production,&nbsp;
managing logistics, and negotiating deals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.840
contractors. But, there was just one problem.&nbsp;
Laws prevented the government from accepting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:43.140
services from unpaid volunteers. But, with that&nbsp;
being said, the government also wasn’t willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:48.240
to pay these executives a substantial salary&nbsp;
because one, they were already rich, and two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.940
this was more of a service than a job. So, they&nbsp;
would eventually settle on paying these executives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:59.940
$1 dollar salaries. Some of the earliest examples&nbsp;
of this were Bernard Baruch, Robert S Brookings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:06.240
and Herbert Bayard Swope who all served on a WW1&nbsp;
advisory commission. This practice just became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.640
even more common during WW2 with even more&nbsp;
notable people participating in the 1 dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:19.080
a year service. Likely the best example of this&nbsp;
was William Knudsen who was the CEO of GM. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:24.060
after the war came to a close and the US entered&nbsp;
one of the most prosperous periods of its history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:31.320
the $1 salary was more or less forgotten about.&nbsp;
And it wasn't until the 1970s that we would see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.980
another resurgence of this trend. The automobile&nbsp;
industry would be hit hard by the 1970s. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.900
was there ludicrous amounts of inflation, but&nbsp;
the Middle East would join forces and impose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.600
a devastating oil embargo on the US and most&nbsp;
western countries. To make things worse, Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:54.600
cars were also starting to gain popularity. The&nbsp;
automotive company that was likely hurt the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.600 --> 00:05:02.400
was Chrysler. By the late 1970s, the company was&nbsp;
on the brink of bankruptcy and was in dire need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:09.180
of a bailout. So, Chrysler’s CEO, Lee Iaccoca,&nbsp;
would begin lobbying Congress for a bailout. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.740
to show that he was serious about turning around&nbsp;
the company and not just pocketing the bailout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:19.440
he would offer to take a $1 salary. Chrysler&nbsp;
would end up receiving the bailout and Iaccoca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.540
would stage one of the biggest turnarounds&nbsp;
the automotive industry has ever seen. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:30.960
the biggest sticking factor for many of his peers&nbsp;
was not his business excellence but his $1 salary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:38.460
You see, back in the 70s and early 80s, taxes&nbsp;
were really bad. In fact, if you made more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:46.020
$215,000 per year, you had to pay income tax&nbsp;
of 70%. But, long term capital gains tax was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:52.800
much more reasonable at 28%. So, for obvious&nbsp;
reasons, founders trended towards decreasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.960
their cash compensation and increasing their&nbsp;
stock compensation. They didn’t take this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:04.740
the extreme of taking $1 salaries, but this is&nbsp;
eventually were it led to with Steve Jobs. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:10.680
Steve Jobs returned to Apple in the late 1990s,&nbsp;
he would accept a $1 salary. He didn’t do this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:17.220
decrease taxes or earn more stock compensation&nbsp;
or really any financial reason. Rather, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:23.160
trying to make a statement that he was there to&nbsp;
save Apple, not to make money. But, it didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:29.850
take long for other CEOs to make this a trend.&nbsp;
From their perspective, this was a win win win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:34.800
win scenario. They could reduce public criticism&nbsp;
about their salaries. Boards could approve stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.620
compensation plans at a magnitude that was simply&nbsp;
impossible with cash. Stock had extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:46.260
growth potential. And founders and CEOs could get&nbsp;
away with paying far less in taxes. Now of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:51.480
these leaders still had to make sure the company&nbsp;
was profitable because that’s what kept that stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:57.960
price high, but this was quickly changing as well&nbsp;
because the next domino to fall were shareholders.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:04.020
SHAREHOLDERS SUCCUMB:
Traditionally, one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.540
attractive features of stock was their dividend.&nbsp;
This makes sense as this is what really makes you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:15.720
feel like an owner of the company. Every quarter,&nbsp;
you get a share of the company’s profits. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:21.600
why many traditional companies like Exxon and Coca&nbsp;
Cola swear by their dividend payments. Exxon has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:27.660
paid dividends for 112 years and they haven’t&nbsp;
even missed a single payment. This isn’t to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:34.560
that Exxon was always performing well. In fact, in&nbsp;
many years like 2020, they were doing quite bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:40.860
but they paid a dividend anyway because&nbsp;
like I said, they swear by it. Historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.700
dividends are what attracted investors&nbsp;
like Warren Buffett to a stock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.300 --> 00:08:00.780
So, maintaining a pristine dividend reputation was&nbsp;
vital for such companies. We should also mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:06.660
that for many stocks, this was their primary&nbsp;
return. I mean steel companies and insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:12.960
companies weren’t gonna double every year. More&nbsp;
realistically, they were gonna grow 5 to 10% for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:19.560
a few decades and then top out. So, the dividend&nbsp;
is what made stocks like AT&amp;T which hasn’t moved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:25.320
in nearly 30 years worth owning. But, all of&nbsp;
this would change with the internet. Suddenly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:31.200
companies were able to grow 100% every year for&nbsp;
10 years straight and then grow 50% every year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:37.320
for the next 10 years. And the company that really&nbsp;
solidified this notion was none other than Amazon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.220
While Amazon’s retail business was never that&nbsp;
profitable, they did find a goldmine called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:49.980
Amazon Web Services. If Jeff Bezos really&nbsp;
wanted to, he could’ve leaned in on AWS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.940
raised the prices of Prime a bit, and&nbsp;
started paying out solid dividends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.940 --> 00:09:00.360
But, this made no sense for our man Jeff. You see,&nbsp;
Jeff had already subscribed to the idea of no cash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:06.900
compensation for himself. He took a very modest&nbsp;
salary of $81,840 throughout his entire career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:13.860
at Amazon. For Jeff, the money was in the stock,&nbsp;
not the bottom line, and he wanted investors to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:19.680
get on the same page. Fortunately for Jeff, there&nbsp;
was a new but rapidly growing subset of investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.020
called growth investors who were getting on the&nbsp;
same page. Most of these investors weren’t fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:30.840
of dividends and they saw dividends as a complete&nbsp;
waste of capital due to taxes. If you didn’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:36.960
dividends are taxed twice, once at the corporate&nbsp;
level, and once at the investor level. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:42.660
that if Amazon was gonna pay $1 in dividends, they&nbsp;
would first have to pay about 25% in corporate tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:47.940
meaning that only 75 cents would make it to the&nbsp;
investor. From there, the investor would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:53.880
to pay personal income tax on that 75 cents. And&nbsp;
given that most investors with a sizable stake are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:59.640
already pretty well off, they usually have to&nbsp;
pay 40% of this dividend to taxes. And yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:05.100
I know that some companies are able to pay tax&nbsp;
exempt dividends, but that’s not always the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:12.840
so bear with me here. With 40% income tax, the&nbsp;
investor is only left with a mere 45 cents. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:18.360
worst part is that many of these investors are&nbsp;
just looking to reinvest the dividends anyway. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:24.000
it makes no sense for Amazon to payout a dividend&nbsp;
only for the government to take 55% and then have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:31.740
45% come back to Amazon. Instead, Amazon can just&nbsp;
reinvest 100% of the profits themselves and that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:37.080
exactly what Amazon has done. For the longest&nbsp;
time, value investors would ridicule Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:43.800
and these so called “growth investors”. But, over&nbsp;
the past 10 years, growth stocks have taken over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:49.140
the market. Most of the big tech companies pay&nbsp;
little to no dividends and all of the returns for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:54.960
investors are from growth. And since then, even&nbsp;
Warren Buffett has opted to invest in Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:59.820
so there was only one more group to&nbsp;
convert to the dark side: employees.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:04.980
EMPLOYEES SUCCUMB:&nbsp;

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:10.560
Like shareholders, employees have historically&nbsp;
trended towards profitable companies. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:16.740
for employees, joining a company isn’t just&nbsp;
an investment, it’s their livelihoods. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:20.820
traditionally, employees used to stick around&nbsp;
at the same company for their entire careers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:26.760
so choosing a stable company with large&nbsp;
profit margins was essential. But, over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:32.280
employees have become more and more risk&nbsp;
tolerant. For one, employees no longer feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:37.380
that they’re tied down to one company. In fact,&nbsp;
they’ve realized that their peers who constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:42.480
switch companies tend to earn a lot more money.&nbsp;
So, employees today are a lot more likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:47.640
join a startup or a company that’s trying to&nbsp;
stage a turnaround. Sure, it might not work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:53.880
but in that case, they can just find another job.&nbsp;
If it does work though, they suddenly become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:59.820
key member at this new company, maybe even an&nbsp;
executive. But, nowadays, you don’t even have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:04.920
to become an executive to cash in on many of the&nbsp;
same perks like stock compensation. Historically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:10.860
employees may have refused to take stock&nbsp;
compensation because it’s inherently riskier. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:15.480
as we said, employees are willing to take&nbsp;
a lot more risk nowadays. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:20.100
they’re a lot more aware of how the wealthy&nbsp;
make their money. They see the Fortune 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:25.380
list, they see billionaires, and they see&nbsp;
YouTube videos like this. And they realize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:31.200
if they want to become truly wealthy, they have&nbsp;
to own assets like real estate and stock. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:37.260
more and more employees started looking and&nbsp;
asking for stock compensation. For companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:42.840
this was a godsend. As we discussed, it’s way&nbsp;
easier for companies to pay stock compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:48.600
than pay cash compensation. This is the main&nbsp;
reason that compensation at big tech companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:56.220
is so high. Unless, you’re at an entry level,&nbsp;
50% or more of the compensation is stock. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:00.960
best part for companies was that they didn’t&nbsp;
even have to convince employees to take stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:08.460
compensation. Employees were literally asking for&nbsp;
it. And with that, the last domino had fallen.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:15.240
MODERN COMPANIES:
None of this is to say that companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:21.780
want to lose money, but they are more tolerant&nbsp;
of losses than ever before. And as long there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:27.660
more growth to achieve, companies are willing to&nbsp;
stay at breakeven operations indefinitely. A great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:33.180
example of this is Salesforce. Every single&nbsp;
year, their revenue has gone up, but their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:38.940
net income is virtually nothing in comparison to&nbsp;
their revenue. A similar story can be seen with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:46.020
Shopify and Amazon and Uber and AirBnB and Zillow&nbsp;
and basically every modern tech company that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:51.600
can think of. As long as the company is growing,&nbsp;
neither the employees nor the founders nor the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:57.060
shareholders care about profit. For all three of&nbsp;
these groups, having the company make no money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:03.360
is counterintuitively the most lucrative option as&nbsp;
no money is being siphoned by taxes and everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:09.300
can be reinvested into further growth. Now, we&nbsp;
should also mention that many of these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:14.940
are starting to realize that there’s no such&nbsp;
thing as infinite growth, but that’s a wholenother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:21.360
story. If you ran a company, would you go after&nbsp;
profit or growth? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:25.260
drop a like if you would like to see more&nbsp;
